-- WSLVault: SUPERSEDED. The work this file described now lives in 029.
-- ** STILL NOT APPLIED — see the .disabled suffix. Do not rename it. **
--
-- Kept rather than deleted because it records a claim that turned out to be
-- wrong in a way that matters, and because the FORCE step at the bottom is the
-- one piece of it that has not shipped.
--
-- ## What moved to 029_rls_enforce.sql
--
--   * Creating `wslvault_app` -> deploy/helm/wslvault/files/bootstrap/rls_app_role.sql.
--     It could not stay in a migration: the migration Job connects as
--     `wslvault`, which has neither SUPERUSER nor CREATEROLE in the live
--     regions, so CREATE ROLE fails there.
--   * The GRANTs and ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES -> 029, guarded on the role
--     existing so the migration is complete either way.
--   * RLS coverage for the tenant-scoped tables 004 and 011 missed entirely
--     (secret_rotations, tenant_quotas, scim_group_members, key_descriptors,
--     tenant_signing_keys, revoked_tokens, sync_jobs, tenant_schemas) -> 029.
--
-- It could not simply be renamed to `019_rls_enforce.sql`, either: migrate.sh
-- applies files once, in name order, by checksum. A new 019 would run before
-- 020-028 on a fresh database and after them on the live ones, so the two would
-- diverge on exactly the tables involved.
--
-- ## The claim that was wrong
--
-- This file said, and 018 said before it, that FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY would
-- "change nothing at all" because `wslvault` is a superuser with BYPASSRLS.
-- That is true of the local docker-compose database, where the postgres image
-- makes POSTGRES_USER a superuser. It is NOT true of the live regions, which
-- run the Bitnami chart. Checked against region A:
--
--     SELECT rolname, rolsuper, rolbypassrls FROM pg_roles;
--      postgres | t | t
--      wslvault | f | f      <-- neither
--
-- `wslvault` is exempt there for one reason only: it owns the tables. So in
-- production FORCE was never a formality — it was the live switch, and applying
-- it "to see if anything changes" would have made every fail-closed policy match
-- zero rows and taken both regions down. The safe-looking step was the dangerous
-- one. That is why 029 does everything EXCEPT force.
--
-- ## What is actually left
--
-- Enforcement does not need FORCE. PostgreSQL applies RLS to any role that is
-- neither the table owner nor a superuser/BYPASSRLS role, so pointing the
-- services at `wslvault_app` is the switch:
--
--     postgresql.appRole.username: wslvault_app
--
-- FORCE is only needed to also constrain `wslvault` itself — the migration Job
-- and anyone at a psql prompt. That is worth having, but it is defence in depth,
-- not the mechanism, and it must come after the switch rather than before it.
--
-- Before setting appRole.username, EVERY service that touches a row-level
-- secured table has to open a scope (wslvault_storage::tenant_scope). Wired so
-- far: secret-engine, replication-agent, lease-manager. Not yet wired:
-- identity-service, crypto-service, transit-engine, policy-engine,
-- sync-scheduler, and the audit writer in wslvault-storage. Flipping the switch
-- before those are done does not raise an error — the policies fail closed, so
-- those services quietly see an empty database. See docs/tenancy.md.

-- =============================================================================
-- The remaining step, for when the switch has been made and has held
-- =============================================================================
-- Removes the owner exemption, so `wslvault` is constrained too. Apply as a new
-- numbered migration (030) at that point, not by renaming this file.

-- ALTER TABLE shared.secrets          FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
-- ALTER TABLE shared.secret_versions  FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
-- ALTER TABLE shared.secret_rotations FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
-- ALTER TABLE shared.leases           FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
-- ALTER TABLE shared.policies         FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
-- ALTER TABLE shared.audit_events     FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
-- ALTER TABLE shared.principals       FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
-- ALTER TABLE shared.api_keys         FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
-- ALTER TABLE shared.scim_users       FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
-- ALTER TABLE shared.scim_groups      FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
--
-- NOTE: the migration Job runs as the owner and would be constrained by this.
-- Any migration that touches tenant rows after 030 must set app.bypass_rls.
